"II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357839} {:answer/id #uuid "384e706b-aaff-56a8-9492-8afb6a575dd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358250} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9660ae-b6a1-5efd-8076-b367d90fa32c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358569} {:answer/id #uuid "84d85faf-24b6-5701-81e3-a44768dd64b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358920} {:answer/id #uuid "f1063b5c-3a9d-58ec-a84b-33db62b2393f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84d85faf-24b6-5701-81e3-a44768dd64b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358920} :answer-field/id #uuid "64a2dcb0-fbbb-518b-bd86-648aad97805e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d79f788-f655-5287-8607-5b096289e0bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-96830" :question/problem "![](images/74/740d578e554cd61bc733079c32c063d41ab4d9dcc775d36f7080ee8e5aa5a622.png)\n\nConsider the function $f:X\\to Y$ given by the mapping diagram shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The domain of $f$ is ${5,10,15}$\n- The codomain of $f$ is ${5,10,12,13,14,15}$\n- The range of $f$ is ${12,13,14}$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nThe range of $f$ is the subset of the codomain that contains all values $y\\in Y$ that are mapped to by at least one element $x\\in X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect our diagram:\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X={5,10,15},$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y={12,13,14},$\n\n- the range of $f$ is ${12,13},$ the subset of elements of $Y$ that have at least one incoming arrow.\n\nAs a result, statements II and III are false, while statement I is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360720 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105893 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca93cd65-65e3-521e-a1e3-2721f45fb6a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Coordinates of an Image Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cfe9812-86f1-5c5b-9fd2-5a6147ed8a40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357859} {:answer/id #uuid "4a8fdc73-f630-52c5-90b5-cff027f2bf0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358389} {:answer/id #uuid "9669aab1-ba94-53d4-b9e7-518f8d8bb0eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359067} {:answer/id #uuid "c49835a5-6cb5-5405-a466-b16bbb77b948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359479} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb47e53-1a29-5602-9773-81346d526d56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c49835a5-6cb5-5405-a466-b16bbb77b948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359479} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2840d95-e615-5d9f-a975-078cc51f7264" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1d4a0e7-8ceb-5003-89cd-d540839fd74d" :question/math-academy-id "q-96855" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(1,-1)$, $(1,0)$ and $(-2,1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$ \n\n- $(10,20)$\n- $(4,-8)$\n- $(-6,6)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 10 \\\\ -12 & -8 & 20\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-6,-12)$, $(-4,-8)$ and $(10,20).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I  only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105893 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca93cd65-65e3-521e-a1e3-2721f45fb6a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Coordinates of an Image Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3495151c-6f9c-5673-88de-f1c56e1af0e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358221} {:answer/id #uuid "5751455e-b08a-59b1-989d-3eba444bfcf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358509} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0bf57c-af5a-58c7-aa89-1d4cb5090cdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359247} {:answer/id #uuid "b7d45304-6eb4-5c6d-b2cb-2ed7cead1fe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359343} {:answer/id #uuid "d49d906c-2287-5d38-a378-e7c13932bd96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7d45304-6eb4-5c6d-b2cb-2ed7cead1fe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359343} :answer-field/id #uuid "80db2f55-379d-536b-89f3-0c339f244b93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b46e610-7522-5441-8d91-b874b0e467ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-96856" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(0,-1),(1,1),$ and $(3,2).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n\n- $(-1,4)$\n- $(-2,4)$\n- $(1,-3)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are  $(-2,4)$, and $(1,-2).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \" II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105893 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca93cd65-65e3-521e-a1e3-2721f45fb6a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Coordinates of an Image Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b22130e-d538-51cd-afa7-dd10f5401224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358688} {:answer/id #uuid "a973aeee-5466-598f-b41a-c22c9961762a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359210} {:answer/id #uuid "abdf9aea-6545-5bd0-9dec-4d467e2cb7cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359229} {:answer/id #uuid "c7316c85-95b4-51ee-bfe4-c004c5b0d590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359505} {:answer/id #uuid "e5d46810-2201-51e7-ade6-257ff2e94041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7316c85-95b4-51ee-bfe4-c004c5b0d590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359505} :answer-field/id #uuid "43f8b74e-fb61-58b8-a354-602b2da35b35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae6d946d-9a9f-5816-82c2-7048c4392f45" :question/math-academy-id "q-96857" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(0,-1),(-2,1),$ and $(3,0).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$ \n\n- $(1,3)$\n- $(0,-3)$\n- $(2,3)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 3 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-3,-9),$  $(1,3)$, and $(3,9).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \" I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360779 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172036 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1343e4de-56ee-563c-bdc8-f8c0cdc54f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Slope Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8df25c87-e636-5dde-b751-e80b8dc57e3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358989} {:answer/id #uuid "90274536-3643-5fef-a31d-ddbe3d4dedb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359009} {:answer/id #uuid "9f1657b5-6491-5a2e-a622-f00650458340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359139} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e65af8-2544-591a-a04c-29a0ec7cd421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359327} {:answer/id #uuid "e718a960-bff2-5a96-b844-4c13a75e05fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8df25c87-e636-5dde-b751-e80b8dc57e3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358989} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a986fe2-482f-5a4b-893a-3449b83a4970" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cae22cc3-a67e-53bb-bb36-eebf3af162ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-97288" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y+1$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $y=-2,$ the slope field has a positive slope\n- At every point along the line $y=-1,$ the slope field is horizontal\n- At every point along the line $y=1,$ the slope field has a negative slope" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,-2)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-2)}=-2+1=-1<0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is negative for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is equal to $-1$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,-1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-1)}=-1+1=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-1)$ is horizontal for any $x.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,1)}=1+1=2>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is positive for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is equal to $2$ for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ca/caa69f6116eba6462c4fe18f2e535d83db350f0797cf1fcd895ac6df9a4d6fd7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172049 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "337518a9-39a1-57e3-8686-74a135f7c3d7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Oriented Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12952cd4-7772-56dc-91d8-15bc8530be70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360911} {:answer/id #uuid "2038019b-1885-5eec-9088-19e1d3928ef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360973} {:answer/id #uuid "2e5ac34d-1889-5353-8d4f-53b2d888139a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361043} {:answer/id #uuid "47758d4f-db43-5767-94a8-6bad79571164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361148} {:answer/id #uuid "c5af9d7e-7ae6-54a3-b951-68572e9e5ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e5ac34d-1889-5353-8d4f-53b2d888139a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361043} :answer-field/id #uuid "9160105b-00f4-5747-a450-ab936f60ad0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0e004a8-576b-5fb6-94f2-3e66cfd74da8" :question/math-academy-id "q-98338" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d1b6a9fa72404e765796d75fc7ba00a391317ef2670e2f65f7c43db32207e8d2.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above is simple, closed, and negatively oriented?" :question/worked-solution "A simple closed curve is positively oriented if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is negatively oriented. \n\nA curve is positively oriented if it is traversed counterclockwise and is negatively oriented if it is traversed clockwise.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the curves:\n\n- Curve I is not closed.\n\n- Curve II is simple, closed, negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\n- Curve III is a simple and closed curve but it is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\n- Curve IV is not simple.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362303 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11372d09-67d9-541a-9fc3-f4d3ea012f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360899} {:answer/id #uuid "21644acb-f019-5b89-bfed-a10b1e66c095" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360979} {:answer/id #uuid "54c407de-2051-50b6-b4c4-b6d4d49eb166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361214} {:answer/id #uuid "c0be473a-9841-5281-b3ad-622f0e51737f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361701} {:answer/id #uuid "e765d40f-d905-59a7-8880-dd026959d10e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54c407de-2051-50b6-b4c4-b6d4d49eb166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361214} :answer-field/id #uuid "9211f8d4-4b04-526a-80d6-74182399c28a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4cdb362d-f774-5da6-bc15-c04006a3144a" :question/math-academy-id "q-98632" :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca296c3a8a2989fd5e5ca647ad4f7d0f4890aa3caf8d9e0a1fce98498d2d7d64.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y+1)^{2}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)^{2}(y+1)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-y-2$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-1$ and $y=2.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-1,2).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=(y+1)^{2},$ and the equilibrium solution is $y=-1$ only.\n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=(y-2)^{2}(y+1).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=(y-2)^{2}(y+1),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-1,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)<0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-y-2.$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=(y+1)(y-2),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-1,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for  $y\\in (-1,2),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation III has the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3aa6c7e0-5267-5b8c-abf2-5d0df309dc2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361103} {:answer/id #uuid "5d74c0da-e225-5209-830d-3e80c0fe6f58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361258} {:answer/id #uuid "830dcc1b-2b2e-5389-b0f1-16feba0089e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361419} {:answer/id #uuid "a6615e8f-f741-5aca-a503-23a2a8928607" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361582} {:answer/id #uuid "b969e418-a210-5467-8aff-175c3f8f7677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d74c0da-e225-5209-830d-3e80c0fe6f58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361258} :answer-field/id #uuid "a39d4165-9cb7-5227-961c-7c53619ef114" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98ee9b06-5212-535a-b3fd-5d1e48bb48f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-98636" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b5afe944331145c5253b2f1100f642b8a91a538295f3c84877eeb86870a04915.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y({y}^{2}-4)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y(4-{y}^{2})$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-4$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-2,$ $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-2,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (0,2).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=y({y}^{2}-4).$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=y(y+2)(y-2),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,0,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (0,2),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-2,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation I has the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=y(4-{y}^{2}).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=y(4-{y}^{2}),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,0,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-4.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)={y}^{2}-4,$ and the equilibrium solutions are $y=\\pm 2$ only.\n\nTherefore, equation III does not have the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362305 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29f574a9-2f08-5294-9f5c-67e219070410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361025} {:answer/id #uuid "3eb87ec8-c295-54ce-86c3-beaa2460079a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361121} {:answer/id #uuid "4e822101-3318-538a-8e55-7c2654b3e80d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361180} {:answer/id #uuid "95c23d54-74fb-5579-9050-5c4e2afeabcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361502} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ce208c-37aa-5358-a431-5805208424b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95c23d54-74fb-5579-9050-5c4e2afeabcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361502} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8e431df-ff4c-55d8-8720-8b0557dc17c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "77dc20d1-343d-5ade-92ad-7f182610538c" :question/math-academy-id "q-98643" :question/problem "![](images/96/96740fdcc0dc6aa22fa097ba714669fae4ef79543aafad72b3fc3db7722f83d1.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y(y-2)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y(y-2)^{2}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+2y$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,0).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=-y(y-2).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=-y(y-2),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=0,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (0,2).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=-y(y-2)^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=-y(y-2)^{2},$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=0,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-y$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $(y-2)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for  $y\\in (-\\infty ,0),$ and $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II has the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}+2y.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=y(y+2),$ and the equilibrium solution are $y=-2,0.$ \n\nTherefore, equation III does not have the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2610a7cf-db25-56cc-a4de-3950f6b93f3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361000} {:answer/id #uuid "ac17eb13-4abf-5033-ad9f-27fd7afee6f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361602} {:answer/id #uuid "c54fbc98-7ef2-5a4b-9aca-02280ca85909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361728} {:answer/id #uuid "dd5fbbcd-aead-5d18-8c6e-26eb82ee8f58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361824} {:answer/id #uuid "f15fd121-a9a5-583b-a3cd-b6da8b635009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c54fbc98-7ef2-5a4b-9aca-02280ca85909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361728} :answer-field/id #uuid "e96588a0-1001-5470-b659-7ae9d6ec24bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "23af5cf8-2a07-56b8-9952-fd82e54a4b1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-98646" :question/problem "![](images/54/54cfcbaa5ce20c1881f35e83bb0ec28fa1399cc5100ee745941ff7bb52ac7705.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-(y+2)^{2}(y-1)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y+2)(y-1)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-(y+2)(y-1)^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=1.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-2,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=-(y+2)^{2}(y-1).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=-(y+2)^{2}(y-1),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,1.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (-2,1).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=(y+2)(y-1).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=(y+2)(y-1),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,1.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}=-(y+2)(y-1)^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=-(y+2)(y-1)^{2},$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,1.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,1)$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-(y+2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $(y-1)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-2,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation III has the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172051 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1af47ea-6253-5d58-9b94-31e52de27106" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Simply-Connected Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c330c16-86a4-5567-97a4-35d7be078109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361318} {:answer/id #uuid "9d0b8f47-f841-5b4b-82f8-d1e9e9169702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361538} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ed1871-6019-50c4-b60d-84897d16cb50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361646} {:answer/id #uuid "be0acb67-df35-5a8d-aed9-550e66100c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361685} {:answer/id #uuid "c528bc6f-a3a3-5b70-81d3-16a2214884c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5ed1871-6019-50c4-b60d-84897d16cb50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361646} :answer-field/id #uuid "cec89a36-df3b-5198-9e47-dea407d04a08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0317a51a-f6d5-582a-aa69-b3f89cc6775d" :question/math-academy-id "q-98690" :question/problem "![](images/0d/0db1568fab302ddbd9f16dbdf9c276eeb62cbac37346b077c1639fe68c2396ac.png)\n\nWhich of the regions shown above are simply connected?" :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the regions:  \n\n- Region I is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region II is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region III is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region IV is not simply connected. Instead, this is a connected region that contains a hole.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362313 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147494 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "680a42fd-9b70-5fb2-916c-d10b71b11d82" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using Phase Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b34748f-857d-5cfa-b351-1a43a20c2692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361107} {:answer/id #uuid "61cb4512-f982-534e-a91e-870ab396ea10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361275} {:answer/id #uuid "84029e23-38de-5dd2-96bf-7030fa4379e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361425} {:answer/id #uuid "c9562f76-31e9-5fb8-8d05-14359ba8542f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361746} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e5787c-1281-5971-b262-071aa37d5e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9562f76-31e9-5fb8-8d05-14359ba8542f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361746} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a69467f-1203-587a-9ced-02e01b856019" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd0fe6f6-6555-578d-880a-04a4380b3213" :question/math-academy-id "q-98700" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4c4ef140c85eb91bd01688cc2a447f13a21c2a51964c6d101887d15045cee95d.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $y=-2$ is a sink\n\nII. $y=-2$ is a source\n\nIII. $y=2$ is a node" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=-2$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points upward. In other words, both arrows point *toward* the point $y=-2.$This means that $y=-2$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=2$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points downward too. In other words, both arrows point in the *same* direction on either side of $y=2.$ This means that $y=2$ is a node.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147494 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "680a42fd-9b70-5fb2-916c-d10b71b11d82" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using Phase Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a74f383-33f8-523e-afc2-28283d62a481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361027} {:answer/id #uuid "352459c2-e87d-555e-a46f-057af18fd807" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361071} {:answer/id #uuid "5762f318-0805-508d-8470-1dccc0ea509e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361234} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb104b9-04bc-5112-9260-37c3ccb3756c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361531} {:answer/id #uuid "f342669d-8a5c-5231-b7f4-b9f8c5b1083e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5762f318-0805-508d-8470-1dccc0ea509e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361234} :answer-field/id #uuid "efc08f17-6874-5b94-858f-d25d69b2fc3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b3fbe51b-8dce-56ec-a759-a07e5a4e34b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-98701" :question/problem "![](images/74/74790c4dbcb2d9cebee802dd155b5fa0a94e442f9bbc59c6277eb0d6fca3d60a.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=-2$ is a source\n- $y=-5$ is a node\n- $y=-5$ is a sink" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=-2$ points upward, and the arrow just below it points downward. In other words, both arrows point *away* from  $y=-2.$ This means that $y=-2$ is a source.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=-5$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points upward. In other words, both arrows point *toward* the point $y=-5.$ This means that $y=-5$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362315 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147494 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "680a42fd-9b70-5fb2-916c-d10b71b11d82" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using Phase Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c3eda40-2242-5e0a-9555-00e5bf887560" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360884} {:answer/id #uuid "593d718c-c9be-5e69-88ba-9ec9c5d045bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361242} {:answer/id #uuid "5d324b6e-bc85-5092-b5ff-97103e5e694b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361254} {:answer/id #uuid "6e99537e-a0e9-5876-b0bb-411dc460d8ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361328} {:answer/id #uuid "8e618e93-96aa-5ed8-a025-fb58a1e0ab02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c3eda40-2242-5e0a-9555-00e5bf887560" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360884} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e1df10d-2460-548d-98f6-05718a45ad84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf8ec5e0-4855-5481-a220-299eb2c89a02" :question/math-academy-id "q-98702" :question/problem "![](images/68/68eb28d31145fe38d481cb2273bc35edc80bfccf1b80b2e9a75b4136eab45629.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=4$ is a source\n- $y=4$ is a node\n- $y=1$ is a sink" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=4$ points upward, and the arrow just below it points downward. In other words, both arrows point *away* from  $y=4.$ This means that $y=4$ is a source.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=1$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points downward too. In other words, both arrows point in the *same* direction on either side oft $y=1.$This means that $y=1$ is a node.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147495 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4535b48e-a33f-5f62-95d3-a5c0991c1f6d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10267759-948c-5e21-b819-eea0a52b9c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360894} {:answer/id #uuid "21c0f117-930c-5f72-bd6c-e01d53d016cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360984} {:answer/id #uuid "8db2a840-a6b6-51d4-8f6c-92bd0dad5406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361466} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c1cb60-43c1-5cc0-a644-c5f45b9d687b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361644} {:answer/id #uuid "c000b11d-6918-5ea4-9dcf-dd56873b8480" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c000b11d-6918-5ea4-9dcf-dd56873b8480" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361695} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e224e7e-dfd1-5c42-9dff-859b4fa1ddef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1d8d74b-55cf-51de-803f-0cecc60a4fb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-98714" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6b9b15bd3c8cda84156d5461fb9f80f892a1dba26151cc37d381757f9f659fa1.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $y=-3$ is a node.\n\nII. $y=2$ is a sink.\n\nIII. $y=2$ is a source." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/36/362ffe8871efa8dc597708b039fa766c3603777ba12460e84d9af1fc39800288.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-3$ is a node.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=2$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362319 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147495 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4535b48e-a33f-5f62-95d3-a5c0991c1f6d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "159d1b6f-bc88-5e81-930b-1feb13f58e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360922} {:answer/id #uuid "3b043f4e-61a3-5d51-8cc8-0b48259e3f97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361106} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd47953-1f4c-5a18-8e96-037941d6ed94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361535} {:answer/id #uuid "b102e3ce-72af-58f5-a986-ba1591632b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361624} {:answer/id #uuid "da8cf20a-84a5-5aef-ab67-755e3689bae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b043f4e-61a3-5d51-8cc8-0b48259e3f97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361106} :answer-field/id #uuid "147b2830-9a9a-5acf-aeaa-a5ee194361a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d67a28ee-e52e-5e0c-b902-450c792552a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-98715" :question/problem "![](images/4a/4a2c0103fb348883b05692721162ffba17758beb0e6f944deb328a506fed8d21.png)\n\nConsiderer the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $y=4$ is a source.\n\nII. $y=2$ is a node.\n\nIII. $y=-1$ is a sink." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/df/df340c40982e6d22ef8a815bf043f84fd78b43b4ddc1763845d03d4b2c793aee.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=4$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement II is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=2$ is a source.\n\n- Statement III is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-1$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362322 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147495 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4535b48e-a33f-5f62-95d3-a5c0991c1f6d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15c32191-765d-58f7-8e7d-323f392118cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360924} {:answer/id #uuid "46e7e4ee-6254-5172-8b9f-397f96825893" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361145} {:answer/id #uuid "a2bc5784-c1de-50bd-9e9e-2bf05ff09080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361564} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f5b8c2-31d6-5107-96f4-ef046c9625b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361842} {:answer/id #uuid "fa87ecf3-4de2-5513-813c-b3aef6e4035f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46e7e4ee-6254-5172-8b9f-397f96825893" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361145} :answer-field/id #uuid "43aa3a0a-d08a-5e61-897e-a608c4281c42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99cb8df0-a236-544f-967a-a702cdee4d6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-98752" :question/problem "![](images/31/3114d1c94b63a2506a66e701346bc7367b901659b78a2cf7c191648c96a716dc.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $y=3$ is a node.\n\nII. $y=-1$ is a sink.\n\nIII. $y=-1$ is a source." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/f7/f72f89ecad74cc708b5950763002757d9d012d3859c7a5bd5eb19ae60fc54e55.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=3$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement II is false while statement III is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-1$ is a source.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133871 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67683e7d-bf4f-5fdf-8fe5-df996c30e836" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Vectors Is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28b3f016-77e0-5fed-9910-f1af71be4795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361014} {:answer/id #uuid "923c31c5-f24c-56e5-be8f-60366c4447f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361483} {:answer/id #uuid "b6732784-8893-5766-95ac-fa9db15d2b07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361648} {:answer/id #uuid "ea92373e-d3c1-5fff-8902-f0e7baa4aecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361881} {:answer/id #uuid "ee81316b-dcb2-53d4-888b-baee2761b9c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28b3f016-77e0-5fed-9910-f1af71be4795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361014} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac4be4ad-6b8f-545c-96a1-edc53b2f47ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "029fdb6a-d6ee-5a1f-bdd5-c75825b67c96" :question/math-academy-id "q-99635" :question/problem "$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(-1)(1)+2(1)(2)+(1)(-3) \\\\  & =(-1)+4-3 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement I is false since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(2)(1)+2(-1)(2)+(2)(-3) \\\\  & =2-4-6 \\\\  & =-8\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{2}⊥̸{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${v}_{2}⊥̸{v}_{3},$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362382 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133871 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67683e7d-bf4f-5fdf-8fe5-df996c30e836" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Vectors Is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f958a7a-8342-5818-894d-1a1679ed3273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361049} {:answer/id #uuid "3c73dd4f-8bbf-52b2-b7cb-80638367ef82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361115} {:answer/id #uuid "7c8dac09-050a-51b6-8b60-aeeec8412286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361388} {:answer/id #uuid "e4748f74-6269-5282-9135-0525fbf8b22a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361859} {:answer/id #uuid "f5d08f73-6553-5647-88fe-d36c62dfa018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c73dd4f-8bbf-52b2-b7cb-80638367ef82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361115} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c915201-703a-540d-b017-3aa7678e7376" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "689e4d4d-2277-5c62-9cd6-9887b81429d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-99656" :question/problem "$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${v}_{2}⊥̸{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =(1)(4)+2(1)(-1)+2(-1)(1) \\\\  & =4-2-2 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(4)(0)+2(-1)(1)+2(1)(1) \\\\  & =0-2+2 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, computing the inner product $⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(1)(0)+2(1)(1)+2(-1)(1) \\\\  & =0+2-2 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\nAs a result, the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362390 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "285c955f-80b8-5793-be98-42fb085c48d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361012} {:answer/id #uuid "51f76ae3-fc53-5ceb-942e-b4248393a927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361197} {:answer/id #uuid "75e99244-27f6-5825-9c3d-c44576dcf7f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361358} {:answer/id #uuid "955c6050-dc9a-5175-b717-2342b379fb03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361498} {:answer/id #uuid "c37db751-9ad5-5430-ab75-5489d9f259e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75e99244-27f6-5825-9c3d-c44576dcf7f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361358} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e95ec87-7b1b-553d-bd1d-6b2645d08b87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06ea44bd-2468-51be-aabc-e31db5c657e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-99815" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={t}^{2},\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{2},{f}_{3}⟩=0.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\cdot {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{4}}{4}-(\\frac{(-\\pi )^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-(-(-\\pi )\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\pi +\\pi +(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362391 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cc59c81-637e-52e7-92da-29524f62b7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361322} {:answer/id #uuid "802fb970-ace7-5f4e-97e6-b37c8d31083d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361407} {:answer/id #uuid "899a8db7-6c1c-587d-911a-d033dfff4c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361448} {:answer/id #uuid "a5985011-8eed-525d-a132-fa5c90200b48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361579} {:answer/id #uuid "c084fbdf-f594-575b-a303-5e655bf5a067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "802fb970-ace7-5f4e-97e6-b37c8d31083d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361407} :answer-field/id #uuid "45b83442-8c98-555a-a0e1-62d1cef63835" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c3132dd-8104-5475-a04a-6ba3130fe419" :question/math-academy-id "q-99822" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={sin}^{2}⁡t,\\,{f}_{3}(t)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,\\pi ]$ of continuous functions over $[0,\\pi ]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{1},{f}_{2}⟩=0.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,1⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨{sin}^{2}⁡t,1⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity ${sin}^{2}⁡\\alpha =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\alpha )}{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[t-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}]{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\pi -\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )}{2}-(0-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\pi -0-(0-0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{2}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{2}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c4a1f08-b0f1-5a1b-acc0-21bb784ad758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360885} {:answer/id #uuid "4bb8d109-8a0a-5cd9-b031-5643137e401f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361165} {:answer/id #uuid "6ec89f85-3d50-5b28-9284-a6ca3b103f85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361329} {:answer/id #uuid "7aa7dda3-d138-5e2f-93cb-6aa10e847b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361379} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c106d9-8717-568d-b8c9-93966eb2cbe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bb8d109-8a0a-5cd9-b031-5643137e401f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361165} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd0740c3-55af-55b0-9072-37fabbd9d3dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30fad2b1-33b1-5011-baca-2c53469ec04d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99827" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=1,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{2},{f}_{3}⟩=0$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi )}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t),$ ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ and ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21aa3b60-7035-52b5-ae28-6a977fededb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360981} {:answer/id #uuid "58c19846-4823-596f-a585-42d6d6d0e029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361239} {:answer/id #uuid "6335a30d-dbe5-5961-82f9-99190d2da6ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361285} {:answer/id #uuid "9912ae7b-51ba-5ed4-bb12-f35d5c8f549d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361519} {:answer/id #uuid "9ee38031-cb20-5a55-be0b-b36494c72614" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9912ae7b-51ba-5ed4-bb12-f35d5c8f549d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361519} :answer-field/id #uuid "18c70965-ac2f-536b-80d9-b8024b87c810" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c64aee5-b8e4-5802-9d88-5d5ccb488483" :question/math-academy-id "q-99839" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,\\pi /2]$ of continuous functions over $[0,\\pi /2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{2},{f}_{3}⟩=0.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+3t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2t)}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)}{4}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )}{4})-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-5t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+5t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-4t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4t)}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)}{6}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi )}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )}{6})-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t),$ ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ and ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b2afcc6-423f-56e3-9376-3f6d5d86a10b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360879} {:answer/id #uuid "1ab956b4-1ee5-50bb-813d-db356da427fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360943} {:answer/id #uuid "3894fd16-9f41-540a-9bfe-664b848e325b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361095} {:answer/id #uuid "561b7b00-721a-5ba0-84d3-1e037016c7d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361225} {:answer/id #uuid "74fb95c8-2498-5319-a7fb-0e6c9083d7cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b2afcc6-423f-56e3-9376-3f6d5d86a10b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360879} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cfa4db9-4fb3-5f43-aab3-4522533819bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8889f645-9334-518f-83dc-7064dd4f1d1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99848" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={t}^{2},\\,{f}_{3}(t)=t-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{3}\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-2,2]$ of continuous functions over $[-2,2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{2},{f}_{3}⟩=0.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}t\\cdot {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}{|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}-(\\frac{(-2)^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,t-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}t\\cdot (t-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}({t}^{2}-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{5}){|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}{2}^{5}-(\\frac{(-2)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}(-2)^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t),$ ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ and ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133871 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67683e7d-bf4f-5fdf-8fe5-df996c30e836" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Vectors Is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "059cde48-5bc6-5673-868b-ea8d54a2ceaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360852} {:answer/id #uuid "0648bfc8-3cf5-525d-952e-35798fa034ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360856} {:answer/id #uuid "071c6897-6b9c-507f-afc9-a42fa5595c82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360861} {:answer/id #uuid "23b3ad14-ea1f-5ed1-8153-b4af930d27f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360989} {:answer/id #uuid "f9448014-3f39-5aa8-ad45-ffec33f870a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "059cde48-5bc6-5673-868b-ea8d54a2ceaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360852} :answer-field/id #uuid "165bbc62-0650-50d6-9912-f876af3735c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "021a928d-5bbc-5ce6-b2a2-f571107e0c32" :question/math-academy-id "q-99875" :question/problem "$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =(1)(1)+3(-1)(3)+2(2)(2) \\\\  & =1-9+8 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(1)(4)+3(3)(0)+2(2)(-1) \\\\  & =4+0-4 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, computing the inner product $⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(1)(4)+3(-1)(0)+2(2)(-1) \\\\  & =4+0-4 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\nAs a result, the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133871 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67683e7d-bf4f-5fdf-8fe5-df996c30e836" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Vectors Is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e459610-671a-55f5-926f-d836642ee1e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360962} {:answer/id #uuid "42a56c57-c027-5da1-a918-c982043c4b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361130} {:answer/id #uuid "92d764c8-5589-5c86-afcb-1260cce729d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361488} {:answer/id #uuid "a80ac5e3-0988-5dff-9337-81973915b4a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361588} {:answer/id #uuid "e52bbe28-55bf-50c5-88e9-b24d3a5bf912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements is true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92d764c8-5589-5c86-afcb-1260cce729d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361488} :answer-field/id #uuid "155818f2-c6ce-5084-8af3-263d5097e9fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a41208f-2a14-503d-a1ad-b859e476f0c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-99878" :question/problem "$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${v}_{1}⊥̸{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =2(1)(5)+(1)(-2)+(2)(-4) \\\\  & =10-2-8 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =2(1)(0)+(1)(2)+(2)(-1) \\\\  & =0+2-2 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, computing the inner product $⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =2(5)(0)+(-2)(2)+(-4)(-1) \\\\  & =0-4+4 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\nAs a result, the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09d64f7c-60c5-5475-9188-10f0ad37b369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360874} {:answer/id #uuid "49ff6a2a-f8f1-536c-9010-d3253c2c863c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361155} {:answer/id #uuid "553acf95-4d47-5e87-b0d5-91b5d4c18302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361219} {:answer/id #uuid "bb691514-1033-5141-87e5-6d94196c1e3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361672} {:answer/id #uuid "bdf8a54d-2d6f-5788-8852-403dfb668e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdf8a54d-2d6f-5788-8852-403dfb668e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361684} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e284079-743c-5982-b9eb-0f2f9cb7833b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d313e454-e9a2-5532-b1b1-37c4d7cbbe80" :question/math-academy-id "q-99951" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)={e}^{t},\\,{f}_{2}(t)=t{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t},\\,{f}_{3}(t)={e}^{-t}\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,2]$ of continuous functions over $[0,2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨{e}^{t},t{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}(t{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-t){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-2-(0-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{e}^{t},{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362675 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b0fb635-b2b2-5e34-ac62-08f8f2e8ae69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362677} {:answer/id #uuid "cb13e4e3-d7df-540c-a097-57428d520b7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362678} {:answer/id #uuid "3bc61d98-b92d-5a97-ae66-d8c9383a525f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362679} {:answer/id #uuid "8d450859-7640-5bcf-b3b8-9063923d9bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362680} {:answer/id #uuid "0a3d8b10-13ac-534a-8c6c-8075fbd6d6b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a3d8b10-13ac-534a-8c6c-8075fbd6d6b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362681} :answer-field/id #uuid "50e44f68-8e03-5772-829b-5a09815b3ae9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92a6911e-532d-5f89-96e6-761d0ceb55f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-109487" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-2I:$\n\n$$\nA-2I=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f322b92-e727-56f8-b965-168eeb7ec238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362684} {:answer/id #uuid "fea449ab-857f-5e97-a2dd-9a5decfbfe3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362685} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e9c614-fb32-5fc3-a8e1-80768c7a6164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362686} {:answer/id #uuid "fd8fd250-9f94-565b-afb8-d6406e6e4d2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362687} {:answer/id #uuid "e4559c11-53ee-582b-ac61-017f9bf594ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f322b92-e727-56f8-b965-168eeb7ec238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362684} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc073027-8d63-5f72-bb4e-ddd774541c01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "83c9089c-246d-51fb-b493-4538acc22376" :question/math-academy-id "q-109574" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A+I:$\n\n$$\nA+I=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $x$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)x=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362710 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ada5d20-89d8-5bc9-9b10-172356be0eb4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Orthogonal Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "501cfa61-1bae-5493-9927-44e080eedcb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362712} {:answer/id #uuid "e79a9ced-86df-5aba-ba5d-8b38b1a30f4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362713} {:answer/id #uuid "d78089f5-2aad-5e43-b5a0-cc7b67658993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362714} {:answer/id #uuid "1fbd0e7e-1a3f-539f-916d-f095722f68be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362715} {:answer/id #uuid "149e462a-2fa3-515b-a582-18f8ee2ca359" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e79a9ced-86df-5aba-ba5d-8b38b1a30f4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362713} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe489986-82fe-5320-9907-156feba7cc97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b12af04-ad27-575e-b0d1-aaf05c9b69e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-109774" :question/problem "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$ for every matrix $A?$\n\n- $\\text{Col}(A)$\n- $\\text{Null}(A)$\n- $\\text{Col}({A}^{T})$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$ is the left null space of $A.$ This consists of vectors that, when pre-multiplied by ${A}^{T},$ result in the zero-vector $0.$\n\nWhen pre-multiplying a vector by ${A}^{T},$ we actually multiply each row of ${A}^{T}$, that is, each column of $A$, by that vector. So, $\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$ must be orthogonal to the column space of $A,$ which is $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/de/de22dd5b352e1d5be48f4b6ed3b2a4657ae623c1bd288d3464559758ea334af7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362717 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ada5d20-89d8-5bc9-9b10-172356be0eb4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Orthogonal Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b69c48-96d4-5148-9d23-458c6d5a1c1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362719} {:answer/id #uuid "87c311c4-ea4d-5db0-b386-be941d6c05b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362720} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ed516f-6983-54ab-b9b1-1326006eaed0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362721} {:answer/id #uuid "5ac3b5cc-540d-5a99-9e0f-4c7bfca468b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362722} {:answer/id #uuid "13649877-0942-5032-9fb2-4c1bcd640b3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8ed516f-6983-54ab-b9b1-1326006eaed0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362721} :answer-field/id #uuid "66be61d8-973a-58fe-b28b-59ce4ae34aa6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90aa20cc-7bd4-5b9c-96fe-e3f55743e36c" :question/math-academy-id "q-109781" :question/problem "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Col}({B}^{T})$ for every matrix $B?$\n\n- $\\text{Col}(B)$\n- $\\text{Null}({B}^{T})$\n- $\\text{Null}(B)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}({B}^{T})$ is the row space of $B.$ This consists of all linear combinations of the rows of $B.$\n\nIf a vector is orthogonal to every linear combination of the rows of $B,$ it must be orthogonal to each row of $B.$\nBut notice that when pre-multiplying a vector by $B,$ we actually multiply each row of $B$ by that vector.\nSo, $\\text{Col}({B}^{T})$ must be orthogonal to the null space of $B,$ which is $\\text{Null}(B).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/18/18713ed3d103ca0a02d74cede1e6448012e0aa28a2df804d41cfb277589a0158.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362757 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ada5d20-89d8-5bc9-9b10-172356be0eb4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Orthogonal Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15ee909a-2213-5908-aab7-710d877e6fa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362759} {:answer/id #uuid "96ddce82-4639-57d8-8962-e06b2b5e382d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362760} {:answer/id #uuid "a3fb9876-21aa-52b8-b681-8d87695f06f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362761} {:answer/id #uuid "3d071a53-691d-52f1-b584-49005877a293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362762} {:answer/id #uuid "d35126b9-8b88-5dc5-b360-043c9494a374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96ddce82-4639-57d8-8962-e06b2b5e382d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362760} :answer-field/id #uuid "db714c4b-ae52-586c-bdd6-a74df8706792" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c3520bf-5736-51de-a6b7-f6ed37ab8f1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109852" :question/problem "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Col}(B)$ for every matrix $B?$\n\n- $\\text{Null}({B}^{T})$\n- $\\text{Col}({B}^{T})$\n- $\\text{Null}(B)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(B)$ consists of all linear combinations of the columns of $B$, that is, the rows of ${B}^{T}.$\n\nIf a vector is orthogonal to every linear combination of the rows of ${B}^{T},$ it must be orthogonal to each row of ${B}^{T}.$\nBut notice that when pre-multiplying a vector by ${B}^{T},$ we actually multiply each row of ${B}^{T}$ by that vector.\nSo, $\\text{Col}(B)$ must be orthogonal to the left null space of $B,$ which is $\\text{Null}({B}^{T}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $B$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/18/18713ed3d103ca0a02d74cede1e6448012e0aa28a2df804d41cfb277589a0158.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362921 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166859 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fba5f68e-15da-5804-b6d9-d72ce6acae59" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field by Identifying Asymptotic Behavior"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8df671d2-4023-5ffb-978c-af5e652520d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362923} {:answer/id #uuid "4ce9e6cf-20b2-5801-af21-b21093705e6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362924} {:answer/id #uuid "8c6b3ad8-6dcd-51f6-84be-d6f2634bb5d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362925} {:answer/id #uuid "d456ec5c-a33c-5318-aafd-9d83a91bd984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362926} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7b6129-0e59-52a8-b7c6-c3d6dc1780a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d456ec5c-a33c-5318-aafd-9d83a91bd984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362926} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dbe725d-2cb3-596a-950a-580b1e6c3920" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "81af22bb-892f-5bfc-b62b-3a9ae21c3aa9" :question/math-academy-id "q-116029" :question/problem "![](images/2b/2b25629c102be82d96b7ca94d5ab5ce499d327837ed53c6b4adb69ee214db81d.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{y-1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}-1}$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/1b/1bf59ce83d7a82899587095217c080a5942f28a9c36c6971ec07ae5e8988e7fc.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to \\pm 1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty )$ and negative for $y\\in (-1,1).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}=\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{1}{y})}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y-1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become vertical as $y\\to -1.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}-1}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\pm \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to \\pm 1.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty )$ and negative for $y\\in (-1,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362976 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166859 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fba5f68e-15da-5804-b6d9-d72ce6acae59" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field by Identifying Asymptotic Behavior"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fb3ca87-7a26-5c72-bfd1-a3fd86c495c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362978} {:answer/id #uuid "a3040ae7-fae0-5e85-934e-b4cff5adf52a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362979} {:answer/id #uuid "98551df9-5584-5920-8feb-90b89f576b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362980} {:answer/id #uuid "7a62cbe7-6315-58e4-9fc5-d1c4b026eef9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362981} {:answer/id #uuid "2be11fb0-e5ce-52b1-99d9-f4ca4b7a5acd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3040ae7-fae0-5e85-934e-b4cff5adf52a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362979} :answer-field/id #uuid "f695614b-55be-556d-b550-3dc61b67861a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1bc4fffb-9fca-5d90-ae58-a105216385f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-120187" :question/problem "![](images/da/da8d6b73685dbd2db82ad6bdaa528f316fb7819ba3f99f226f0ad9127d3a5609.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{y}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y+1}$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/9b/9b6c8c113f59cc006f26e336b85abde6337a3af2a9f5d6dccc001eee636991ac.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive for $y<0$ and negative for $y>0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{y}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\pm \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to 0.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for $y<0$ and negative for $y>0.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are positive for $y>0.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y+1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{y}{y+1}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363405 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48472de6-866a-5dcd-a9f6-02aa1ff0cdd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363407} {:answer/id #uuid "04d2d6ac-d98c-5684-8ea1-3861da991c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363408} {:answer/id #uuid "62323ad1-50a6-5ba0-a1c5-61054e5c619e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363409} {:answer/id #uuid "afb7d291-f5c3-524d-85de-5281265ce3a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363410} {:answer/id #uuid "5173d7df-b3a7-583d-9b15-3abc62346e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04d2d6ac-d98c-5684-8ea1-3861da991c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363408} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb7abbf5-d5b5-5cf3-9ed4-ede86c5ffb43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ef54c03e-38cc-58aa-92b0-b3a91ad36505" :question/math-academy-id "q-158422" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f955ee719a4e69f0d09c678f0a74023f137efd7b5ae36d49b98706156384173.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{1}{2},\\, & 0<x<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}]-[\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{0}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363619 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ade459-9aba-5520-95d3-69ef3fd62d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a52b768-c3eb-5bde-97e5-a532bb77dd1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363621} {:answer/id #uuid "aea978a8-7a1f-5511-b2ce-7a327be58b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363622} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d62003-b1b3-5199-ab7b-bd7885ae44f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363623} {:answer/id #uuid "2089dcc9-62c8-5743-b2e9-f8f2a0245131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363624} {:answer/id #uuid "a9356a0a-ab16-525d-843d-77e76c4b9e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aea978a8-7a1f-5511-b2ce-7a327be58b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363622} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f8a348d-8f04-54bc-82e3-cb2e42565377" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "875338da-f85c-5b2a-9645-5f7fb1d7affd" :question/math-academy-id "q-253466" :question/problem "Which of the following recurrence relations are first-order?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=4{a}_{n-1}+7$\n- ${b}_{n}={b}_{n-1}+3{b}_{n-3}$\n- ${c}_{n}=5{c}_{n-1}-2n$" :question/worked-solution "The order of a recurrence relation is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${a}_{n-1}$ in the relation is $1.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $1.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${b}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${b}_{n-1}$ and ${b}_{n-3}$ in the relation is $3.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $3.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${c}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${c}_{n-1}$ in the relation is $1.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363626 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ade459-9aba-5520-95d3-69ef3fd62d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00c37177-1257-5361-8a1c-1fc4165b7598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363628} {:answer/id #uuid "70827be5-0f7a-5a2a-a291-73022cfe7931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363629} {:answer/id #uuid "d041ba80-d063-556b-a0c8-4703b661b3bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363630} {:answer/id #uuid "e7462c0d-a120-5966-9bcb-c93c95bb2e8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363631} {:answer/id #uuid "eb50e688-1d97-5697-8b97-e6a866a40cc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb50e688-1d97-5697-8b97-e6a866a40cc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363632} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb00d54e-bf9e-5084-92c5-709b87a491e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18b3d391-1187-5c01-902c-37defcaf71f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-253483" :question/problem "Which of the following recurrence relations are linear (with constant coefficients)?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=-n{a}_{n-1}+4{a}_{n-2}$\n- ${b}_{n}=5{b}_{n-1}-2n$\n- ${c}_{n}={c}_{n-1}^{2}-3{c}_{n-2}$" :question/worked-solution "A recurrence relation is **linear (with constant coefficients)** if it expresses ${a}_{n}$ as a sum of previous terms ${a}_{n-1},…,{a}_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere ${s}_{1},{s}_{2},…,{s}_{k}$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a **forcing function**, that does not involve ${a}_{n}$ or its preceding terms. \n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- ${a}_{n}$ is not linear. The coefficient of the term $-n{a}_{n-1}$ is not constant.\n\n- ${b}_{n}$ is linear. The term ${b}_{n-1}$ occurs only in the first power with constant coefficient.\n\n- ${c}_{n}$ is not linear. The term ${c}_{n-1}^{2}$ is not in the first power.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363633 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ade459-9aba-5520-95d3-69ef3fd62d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb32dc55-377d-5645-83e9-876711297904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363635} {:answer/id #uuid "91bbf08d-b47b-5e5d-a78e-e5211955f623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363636} {:answer/id #uuid "d744fc50-e35c-5913-9fc1-a1796b3ba201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363637} {:answer/id #uuid "da1806d2-0921-560c-81ba-ef6862fc87c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363638} {:answer/id #uuid "e97764b9-b518-5d91-aa47-a19a56d29e8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e97764b9-b518-5d91-aa47-a19a56d29e8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363639} :answer-field/id #uuid "229ea531-5a5f-59ff-ab5e-ac3edbc0063c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c27291fe-59f1-5d75-bef8-47b51d3e493f" :question/math-academy-id "q-253486" :question/problem "Which of the following linear recurrence relations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${a}_{n}={a}_{n-1}-3{a}_{n-2}+n$\n- ${b}_{n}={b}_{n-1}+5{b}_{n-2}$\n- ${c}_{n}=6{c}_{n-1}+1$" :question/worked-solution "A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is **homogeneous** if the forcing function is zero. \n\nIn other words, the linear recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n),\n$$\n\nis homogeneous if $g(n)=0$ for all $n,$ and ${s}_{i}$ is constant for each $i.$\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- ${a}_{n}$ is not homogeneous. The term $n$ of the expression doesn't contain preceding terms of the relation.\n\n- ${b}_{n}$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, ${b}_{n-1}$ and ${b}_{n-2}.$\n\n- ${c}_{n}$ is not homogeneous. The term $1$ of the expression doesn't contain preceding terms of the relation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ade459-9aba-5520-95d3-69ef3fd62d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80fd47be-8132-50cb-9d8b-08c4d4394cfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363642} {:answer/id #uuid "735c82e6-45c4-5706-9f50-1ab3bc928262" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363643} {:answer/id #uuid "0e6160e3-97b0-56a4-9692-ae2dafd6d449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363644} {:answer/id #uuid "2beb2a54-da9a-5ab3-8b36-4c8001f183ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363645} {:answer/id #uuid "18443de6-d3d5-50a5-89ca-48ad2614670f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80fd47be-8132-50cb-9d8b-08c4d4394cfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363642} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0885c86-fd77-5621-bbd1-06d43262486d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8e46c1db-beb9-52c4-8f72-f2f2edfce45c" :question/math-academy-id "q-253487" :question/problem "Which of the following linear recurrence relations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{3}{a}_{n-1}-\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{n-2}+2$\n- ${b}_{n}=5{b}_{n-1}-{b}_{n-2}$\n- ${c}_{n}=\\frac{1}{4}{c}_{n-1}$" :question/worked-solution "A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is **homogeneous** if the forcing function is zero. \n\nIn other words, the linear recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n),\n$$\n\nis homogeneous if $g(n)=0$ for all $n,$ and ${s}_{i}$ is constant for each $i.$\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- ${a}_{n}$ is not homogeneous. The term $2$ of the expression doesn't contain preceding terms of the relation.\n\n- ${b}_{n}$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, ${b}_{n-1}$ and ${b}_{n-2}.$\n\n- ${c}_{n}$ is homogeneous. The term of the expression contains a preceding term, ${c}_{n-1}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28939f65-9fd6-564d-a0c2-73c1038ac898" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a783a885-59c4-5045-beb2-e5658ca14227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364042} {:answer/id #uuid "88e9116b-0b9b-5e63-b54e-568a9df1977a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364043} {:answer/id #uuid "8163399c-99cf-5a72-8428-0db4c63be287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364044} {:answer/id #uuid "1cf9b4a4-21cc-50f3-8c85-1ba421aa6c0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364045} {:answer/id #uuid "6521e768-5e2c-5726-bfee-f504aa6755e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cf9b4a4-21cc-50f3-8c85-1ba421aa6c0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364045} :answer-field/id #uuid "034a22cb-0966-55b2-9f99-48d3fa6d6fe8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b1ece1ec-19c9-5790-a759-998564647939" :question/math-academy-id "q-339659" :question/problem "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)$" :question/worked-solution "In matrix form, the system\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=A(t)x(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nis *homogeneous* when $f≡0,$ and *inhomogeneous* when $f≢0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is inhomogeneous since $f=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]≢0.$\n\n- System II is inhomogeneous since $f=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]≢0.$\n\n- System III is homogeneous since $f≡0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364047 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28939f65-9fd6-564d-a0c2-73c1038ac898" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64ae9f36-2ae5-5b74-995c-1a03435d7d84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364049} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f1679e-4ba9-5cd2-891c-6921b7d46b96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364050} {:answer/id #uuid "95cdb787-8d2a-5203-b629-98f7c0091c2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364051} {:answer/id #uuid "9d213c59-8c57-5a0a-9578-64e2c4240cb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364052} {:answer/id #uuid "861bd730-7fe3-5b41-a5ab-caaeb8427723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5f1679e-4ba9-5cd2-891c-6921b7d46b96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364050} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3900507-4225-54f1-a763-617ded408cb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "73f3b368-2531-52b2-833e-f683b0622933" :question/math-academy-id "q-339665" :question/problem "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+5\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=7{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of the form\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f≡0$ and $g≡0$ (i.e., both $f$ and $g$ are zero functions), then the system is *homogeneous*. Otherwise, if at least one of $f$ or $g$ is not identically zero, the system is *inhomogeneous*.\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≡0$ and $g≡0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=3, &  & b(t)=2, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=-1, &  & d(t)=4, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System II is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $g≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=-1, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=-2, &  & d(t)=0, &  & g(t)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System III is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≡0$ and $g≡0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=0, &  & b(t)=0, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=7, &  & d(t)=3, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5f07bf4-4a80-5c38-8806-6cbd8d26fa57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364147} {:answer/id #uuid "862b97ed-b399-5806-9f72-3a71bd6e9f7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364148} {:answer/id #uuid "557f525c-a15e-58a6-9656-27d5203e43a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364149} {:answer/id #uuid "a79d8796-f65e-5d31-bb2f-29124bc7fccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364150} {:answer/id #uuid "09b175a4-227d-55cd-a19d-33c07569f7d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a79d8796-f65e-5d31-bb2f-29124bc7fccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364150} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea5cc0e6-6d5a-59ee-82ed-ff12bda5ecf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e3fcd83b-c9bf-54f3-97b2-1811800f0c15" :question/math-academy-id "q-38889" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{7}\\to {R}^{7}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2}$ vectors such that $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})$, then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}$\n\nII. $T$ is invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{nullity}(T)>0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{7}$ into ${R}^{7},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=7 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$\n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$ In turn, this means that $\\text{nullity}(T)>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c52cc7b6-9bfa-5536-9e5f-59eeb0900797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364215} {:answer/id #uuid "b4ecdf86-13ef-5e0a-b4f9-6f8533300313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364216} {:answer/id #uuid "9ed48d52-d008-59d3-a693-7857aac6b5e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364217} {:answer/id #uuid "512e4185-0fa5-516c-8c01-749de4125a44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364218} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ba01e1-6325-586c-aa39-fa54eae6601b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ed48d52-d008-59d3-a693-7857aac6b5e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364217} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad2b2b69-4210-5e1c-81b8-a09e94838b9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3c1b6231-ddc6-5ac3-a5c8-9d11b32158e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-47456" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(T).$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If ${v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=0$, then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$\n- $\\text{rank}(T)=3$\n- $T$ is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since the columns of $T$ satisfies ${v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=0$, then they are linearly dependent, which gives us $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Therefore, according to the Invertible Matrix Theorem, we must have $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3},$ the transformation $T$ can't be a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aee17e3a-031d-5c0e-9488-17410b3a2372" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Dimensions of Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e105f0b-e1ce-57ea-93e5-f9c2602d9ed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364725} {:answer/id #uuid "db73441a-015d-5b78-9270-03e1141027fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364726} {:answer/id #uuid "7795e9b1-b217-5dc6-b733-6518146b83fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364727} {:answer/id #uuid "a00c46a1-8ee4-5ff8-a095-c8d32431591c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364728} {:answer/id #uuid "cbd9c344-617e-577a-869e-ac013d60022d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db73441a-015d-5b78-9270-03e1141027fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364726} :answer-field/id #uuid "deab58b3-dd37-5659-a248-9f3e15b35602" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a2340ea-4d69-565f-a0a6-1a2327573430" :question/math-academy-id "q-106904" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $11?$\n\n- ${R}_{10}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $10$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{2\\times 6}(R),$ the vector space of all $2\\times 6$ matrices over $R$\n- ${R}^{10}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{10}[t])=10+1=11.\\,✓\n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{2\\times 6}(R))=2\\cdot 6=12\\ne 11.\\,\\times \n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{10})=10\\ne 11.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364730 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e609aabf-7e7e-5997-b8dc-c66e7a14a36f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Finite and Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60dd5158-d283-5453-8d5f-8930bf63cd91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364732} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a04cf5-0c3a-5245-8c4b-860f3ac9416c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364733} {:answer/id #uuid "00babb9c-00d3-5963-90bb-09bcb833a581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364734} {:answer/id #uuid "0a1d2efc-04c3-5a01-842a-a45dbaa561a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364735} {:answer/id #uuid "a4ab6ab6-a954-5fc9-9cb2-6c09f0a46016" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60dd5158-d283-5453-8d5f-8930bf63cd91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364732} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a8e9c59-4db2-5da4-aa1b-9e9025a98565" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ad8a280-94c8-5978-909a-9e22277ca8ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-106918" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\n- $D(R),$ the vector space of all differentiable functions on $R$\n- ${R}^{100}$\n- ${R}_{2000}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2\\,000$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- $D(R)$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following subset of $D(R)$ is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,{t}^{n}},\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{x}_{i}{t}^{i}=0\n$$\n\nfor all $t\\in R$ if and only if ${x}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of $D(R)$  of size $n,$ which imples that $D(R)$ cannot have finite dimension.     .\n\n- ${R}^{100}$ and ${R}_{2000}[t]$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{100})=100\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{2000}[t])=2000+1=2\\,001,\n$$\n\nare both finite numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172029 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a6bbb34-a43f-5e16-8a24-25c0d6970a38" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type From Its Reduced Equation: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebbc61b3-da42-52b4-8b9a-1c8bfc6e51d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365012} {:answer/id #uuid "e57b11cb-0a00-5658-98f3-d87f6a7775f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365013} {:answer/id #uuid "259d1b4b-cb8c-5458-acca-8cb674f94596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365014} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d2ca84-53fb-576d-85e4-ed21ca559e67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365015} {:answer/id #uuid "088b9f24-a8df-521a-bfbe-dbaa748a06e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebbc61b3-da42-52b4-8b9a-1c8bfc6e51d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365012} :answer-field/id #uuid "713649dd-841d-595d-ad2c-1d706d7c2e1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7ec5e81-6115-5881-b6f7-16bb9226da5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-140554" :question/problem "Which of the following equations define a non-empty set of points in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane?\n\n- ${x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}=-1$\n- ${x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}=0$\n- ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the equations in turn.\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}=-1$ defines a non-empty set. It represents the hyperbola ${x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{1}^{2}=1.$\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}=0$ defines a non-empty set. It represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the negative ${x}_{2}$-axis in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=0$ defines a non-empty set. It represents a single point $(0,0)$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365603 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "276898e7-57a4-5ca6-8df7-99fd318a2702" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decoupled System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ecdc87a-c565-5163-94b6-cf505630846d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365605} {:answer/id #uuid "46c4174c-aa3e-55f8-92f7-9d297693180d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365606} {:answer/id #uuid "4b90e9b1-c987-5d5c-9dd6-5a83cecd1d2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365607} {:answer/id #uuid "ec1a127d-dd8a-5db2-b7d1-b71ac9c14069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365608} {:answer/id #uuid "39f72397-ab04-549b-8b5d-7c41730005e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39f72397-ab04-549b-8b5d-7c41730005e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365609} :answer-field/id #uuid "324e36a2-8799-5a66-9f08-fe3fa316e513" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b1bf4017-8b21-55a6-92c8-e49841f3879b" :question/math-academy-id "q-331092" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)$" :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently.  \n\nIn matrix form, a linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ is decoupled if $A$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System II is decoupled since its corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\n- Systems I and III are *not* decoupled because their matrices are not diagonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366039 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39a93edd-ad31-531a-b11d-331e83744aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366041} {:answer/id #uuid "0a72ba19-8116-5007-a175-93b746866f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366042} {:answer/id #uuid "78e13641-9025-567e-838f-cff98c736bdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366043} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8c94f6-289c-5031-af4e-977788f3dfef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366044} {:answer/id #uuid "8c70843d-666b-57fc-87c0-bf584dd95392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c70843d-666b-57fc-87c0-bf584dd95392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366045} :answer-field/id #uuid "1713e681-b157-5242-b361-ddd94b6260d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "006b1b75-818e-5ba9-8f81-bc35ede44097" :question/math-academy-id "q-38141" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $\\text{rank}(T)=2,$ then $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$\n- If $[-2,0,0]^{T}$ is a solution of $T(x)=0$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$\n- If $T(x)=[1,0,0]^{T}$ has no solution then $\\text{rank}(T)=3$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is a solution of $Tx=0$, we have that $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and therefore $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $T(x)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has no solution, we have that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Hence, $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba9d2401-4e30-5327-b8eb-b4db7108dd0c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Linear Transformations in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b627793-3225-5deb-92ee-2435f9cfe821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366054} {:answer/id #uuid "8041e009-04fa-554e-a103-43a41c4be622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366055} {:answer/id #uuid "57205720-729e-5df5-b191-b16752215689" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366056} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d07d55-84fe-5299-bf65-f860acba1c1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366057} {:answer/id #uuid "46b993d9-0c06-5c28-8e26-7ee0692c4652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46b993d9-0c06-5c28-8e26-7ee0692c4652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366058} :answer-field/id #uuid "4adfa4bb-a01a-5ffe-bd19-be00b56b101d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "34581764-4087-5b06-9eff-4679fe703405" :question/math-academy-id "q-38300" :question/problem "If $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=-3x$ then, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨1,3⟩$ belongs to the range of $T$\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- $\\text{Im}(T)={0}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is a one-dimensional line, then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=1\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. \nNotice that the point $(1,3)$ does not satisfy the equation of the line $y=-3x$ since $3\\ne -3\\cdot 1.$ Therefore, the vector $⟨1,3⟩\\notin \\text{Im}(T).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \nsince $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line passing through the origin, we have that the standard matrix $T$ is singular. Hence, $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. \nFor example,  the point $(1,-3)$ satisfies the equation  $y=-3x$ since $-3=-3\\cdot 1.$ Therefore, the vector $⟨1,-3⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T).$ Thus, $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {0}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba9d2401-4e30-5327-b8eb-b4db7108dd0c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Linear Transformations in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ad82c63-09eb-5db5-a423-cb22d7590b06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366061} {:answer/id #uuid "77a61faf-4e00-545a-b2e4-e704769e3e06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366062} {:answer/id #uuid "e857bdb4-10de-5d73-aae4-c628eae4e59e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366063} {:answer/id #uuid "724a5c19-9e50-5e69-92f1-fa998b538696" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366064} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ff393d-6aa6-52e0-80d1-5e6793a9819d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5ff393d-6aa6-52e0-80d1-5e6793a9819d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366065} :answer-field/id #uuid "1718619d-4a2a-5ce8-936e-96766764ba24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "07bd3910-da00-5269-ba42-c4e894fc0a77" :question/math-academy-id "q-38305" :question/problem "If $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $5y=6x$ then, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨\\frac{6}{5},1⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T)$\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is a one-dimensional line, then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=1\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false.\nNotice that the point $(\\frac{6}{5},1)$ does not satisfy the equation of the line $5y=6x$ since $5\\cdot 1\\ne \\frac{6}{5}\\cdot 6.$ Therefore, \n$⟨\\frac{6}{5},1⟩\\notin \\text{Im}(T).$\n\n- Statement II is true.\nSince $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line passing through the origin, we have that the standard matrix $T$ is singular. So, $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement III is false.\nSince  statement II is true we have that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim(Ker}(T))\\ne 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52261ad5-06ec-5820-b379-2fa7e2076f68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366099} {:answer/id #uuid "2adddfdb-1396-5670-af1d-5f244b91a536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366100} {:answer/id #uuid "18a44539-28f0-5861-b3bf-f00b77964913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366101} {:answer/id #uuid "8e23dce7-f9e9-528b-b5da-facf341c2566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366102} {:answer/id #uuid "aedc4dae-4a5e-589f-b565-ed70c37e8829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52261ad5-06ec-5820-b379-2fa7e2076f68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366099} :answer-field/id #uuid "969eea6e-2298-5a9b-b572-aaf060b2e3e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "936f721f-93be-5c51-8233-7da49b500101" :question/math-academy-id "q-38979" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $\\text{Ker}(T)={⟨x,y,z⟩\\,|\\,x+y=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)+1=n$\n- If there exists a vector $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ then $|T|\\ne 0$\n- If ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent and $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})=0$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{n}$ into ${R}^{n},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Solving $x+y=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=-y.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-y \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=y{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+z{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(T)+2 & =n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T)$ then $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{n}$, so, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $T$ is not a bijection. Thus, the matrix $T$ is not invertible, and, consequently, $|T|=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})=0$, then ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$ But the vectors are linearly independent, therefore, the dimension of  $\\text{Ker}(T)$ is at least $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e48f08ab-e789-52b5-90a4-2c5c3b1dec2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366429} {:answer/id #uuid "66559173-9f8e-5a08-9ea5-6edc898fe959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366430} {:answer/id #uuid "7b616462-d520-5e6e-997d-be197c87b620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366431} {:answer/id #uuid "7e5c2d6d-eb93-517f-a9d8-9411a7f6cf62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366432} {:answer/id #uuid "fd87133a-e8f2-5b48-9a50-07332a6fc47d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e5c2d6d-eb93-517f-a9d8-9411a7f6cf62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366432} :answer-field/id #uuid "87c146b7-c44d-5014-a4a1-646dc9ff420e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f6a64ec-417d-5f3c-a079-819cbc656ca3" :question/math-academy-id "q-83440" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and let ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ be a set of linearly independent vectors in ${R}^{3}.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If  $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$, then $\\text{__________}.$ *\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$\n- $\\text{rank}(T)=1$\n- $T$ is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ and the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\text{nullity}(T)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3$ (or since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$), the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da20ffe7-173b-5fc0-8629-3b9b70222860" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca1c9011-4ae6-5782-bddd-b9bf7a3e38b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366485} {:answer/id #uuid "61914b14-f5b4-52cc-9c62-15192bb09db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366486} {:answer/id #uuid "a31df482-5dfb-5c2d-8201-f46fa3b37265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366487} {:answer/id #uuid "0005a8ea-913e-5b7b-b095-e71af063b627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366488} {:answer/id #uuid "b5033569-8b16-5f3d-bb71-10e07bf5a1d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca1c9011-4ae6-5782-bddd-b9bf7a3e38b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366485} :answer-field/id #uuid "d824b74c-9e1f-5141-804b-27c8039bcb40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e6401598-f82b-58db-b408-23496dde6519" :question/math-academy-id "q-84344" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrices $P$ and $Q?$\n\n- $2PQ$ is always orthogonal\n- $P-Q$ is always orthogonal\n- $P$ is **not** invertible" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the product $(2PQ)\\cdot (2PQ)^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2PQ)\\cdot (2PQ)^{T} & =2PQ\\cdot 2{Q}^{T}{P}^{T} \\\\  & =4P(Q{Q}^{T}){P}^{T} \\\\  & =4P(I){P}^{T} \\\\  & =4I \\\\  & \\ne I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the following subtraction of two orthogonal matrices:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe resulting matrix is not orthogonal since, for example, its first column is not normalized.\n\n- Statement III is false. The inverse of an orthogonal matrix $P$ is given by ${P}^{-1}={P}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da20ffe7-173b-5fc0-8629-3b9b70222860" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e52ee037-64df-55a7-b783-d32c998fe3ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366492} {:answer/id #uuid "04f54020-5ff2-5ad2-afc8-663e0a9e5727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366493} {:answer/id #uuid "84196f39-0db9-58f2-9bc2-f0718b6e1e3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366494} {:answer/id #uuid "b88d42a1-4ee6-55f0-a80d-0a04d29989d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366495} {:answer/id #uuid "a01565f9-4951-5ba5-a2e2-3ba4906940bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04f54020-5ff2-5ad2-afc8-663e0a9e5727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366493} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4596c93-1f98-56fd-be89-7326232e9627" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dcdd2917-752d-5fd1-ac2a-6a285cbe6a4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-84353" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrices $P$ and $Q?$\n\n- $Q$ is invertible\n- ${P}^{T}$ is orthogonal\n- ${Q}^{-1}$ is always orthogonal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The inverse of an orthogonal matrix $Q$ is given by ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the product ${P}^{T}\\cdot ({P}^{T})^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{T}\\cdot ({P}^{T})^{T} & ={P}^{T}\\cdot P \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Computing the product ${Q}^{-1}\\cdot ({Q}^{-1})^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{-1}\\cdot ({Q}^{-1})^{T} & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot ({Q}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={Q}^{T}Q \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366553 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e2ef4e8-4bc8-5f22-bcea-26f300695fa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366555} {:answer/id #uuid "aff63ab0-12c1-505c-b7b8-b899c34bdaf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P,$ $Q,$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366556} {:answer/id #uuid "d632b7d7-7d0f-5b18-bdc5-73cc9f693c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366557} {:answer/id #uuid "da92ebc4-1e7b-50f9-b430-bacb3c007ec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366558} {:answer/id #uuid "02d02b66-896a-59c9-ab06-541e83d26052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366559}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02d02b66-896a-59c9-ab06-541e83d26052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366559} :answer-field/id #uuid "d40a1f3b-b768-5864-948b-133ae0173b2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c1ef725-7db0-58fe-a7db-0097c019b6b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-93987" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0.5 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0.5 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366560 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37f0f58d-6200-5015-b95e-2a64465d45eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366562} {:answer/id #uuid "3116cfe4-f0aa-52cf-a243-91123481e80e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366563} {:answer/id #uuid "98fa28e5-8900-569f-8244-1738cf4d3337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366564} {:answer/id #uuid "aa33aee1-db89-5bc9-a347-03c8491c0e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366565} {:answer/id #uuid "d2dfd12e-4619-5cda-89cb-dea84f9591ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa33aee1-db89-5bc9-a347-03c8491c0e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366565} :answer-field/id #uuid "edf5fcb7-d0b1-53f3-bb5e-18cea24a5ecf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366561}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a97d726e-d379-5097-84d8-abb6b0a769af" :question/math-academy-id "q-93992" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{23}\\ne {q}_{32},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e609aabf-7e7e-5997-b8dc-c66e7a14a36f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Finite and Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f517337-3486-5e41-9b39-0c180220781a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366610} {:answer/id #uuid "4dfab1e2-c1de-50ed-8416-0b78f6b3fd0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366611} {:answer/id #uuid "3dc9d420-6eff-5ee6-af41-08b96ceba9f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366612} {:answer/id #uuid "61bd780b-bc33-5556-869d-03c7ee8e861e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366613} {:answer/id #uuid "437a131a-8465-554b-9149-de725c2e5fb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dfab1e2-c1de-50ed-8416-0b78f6b3fd0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366611} :answer-field/id #uuid "d66b6070-cabf-55e5-9e72-a374aef6d9b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd51b750-1389-5155-b65d-c0946321f8d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-95388" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\n- $D(R),$ the vector space of all differentiable functions on $R$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{10\\times 100}(R),$ the vector space of all $10\\times 100$ matrices over $R$\n- ${R}_{200}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $200$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- $D(R)$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following subset of $D(R)$ is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,{t}^{n}},\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{x}_{i}{t}^{i}=0\n$$\n\nfor all $t\\in R$ if and only if ${x}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of $D(R)$  of size $n,$ which imples that $D(R)$ cannot have finite dimension.     .\n\n- ${\\text{M}}_{10\\times 100}(R)$ and ${R}_{200}[t]$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{10\\times 100}(R))=10\\cdot 100=1\\,000\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{200}[t])=200+1=201,\n$$\n\nare both finite numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172029 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a6bbb34-a43f-5e16-8a24-25c0d6970a38" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type From Its Reduced Equation: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74284b82-6c00-50fb-87d1-3ddbcaf76ea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366720} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f287e6-fc5a-5612-b6dc-6230a07c50e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366721} {:answer/id #uuid "c06a6c66-9a0d-5f1b-9652-7f1a9fa3de3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366722} {:answer/id #uuid "104fff1c-e7d5-577f-9a3f-09e2515d3988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366723} {:answer/id #uuid "8de9089d-d7bc-5109-9499-41d65a155982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c06a6c66-9a0d-5f1b-9652-7f1a9fa3de3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366722} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b490a57-fb54-5a0a-88db-c6476bcf4020" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66610d66-38e7-5d9e-b883-9467a5f6ad73" :question/math-academy-id "q-95897" :question/problem "Which of the following equations define a non-empty set of points in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ plane?\n\n- $2{x}_{2}^{2}+1=0$\n- $-2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}=2$\n- ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}=0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the equations in turn.\n\n- The equation $2{x}_{2}^{2}+1=0$ defines an empty set. Notice that the left-hand side is positive for all ${x}_{2},$ while the right-hand side is zero.\n\n- The equation $-2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}=2$ defines an empty set. Notice that the left-hand side is non-positive for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ while the right-hand side is  positive.\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}=0$ defines a non-empty set in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane. It represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the negative ${x}_{2}$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115334 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf19e2ee-7c42-58b0-9254-5a44f315288a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Block Diagonal and Block Triangular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69d36ff0-1b22-50b5-af1f-216011cbce4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366947} {:answer/id #uuid "71adef4e-c668-593f-bf0e-363de3313319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366948} {:answer/id #uuid "abf3e242-bc6f-51cb-b1a1-baaccec73360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366949} {:answer/id #uuid "c9487d7a-239c-541e-a823-dd26d4bfdfb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366950} {:answer/id #uuid "c4e4ccef-1729-57af-8c64-b5c59745921b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4e4ccef-1729-57af-8c64-b5c59745921b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366951} :answer-field/id #uuid "04ff00ee-7b3e-55f0-8fb1-096561394844" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3da29d09-2c10-59b3-aafb-a3f2d64ea706" :question/math-academy-id "q-109246" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are block upper triangular?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A block upper triangular matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and the blocks below the main diagonal contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$is *not* a block upper triangular matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* a block upper triangular matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $C$ is a block upper triangular matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367338 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7eb0a67d-d925-5d22-b428-c6a5e1df2532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367340} {:answer/id #uuid "57d49a75-850a-580c-911f-1b7521839673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367341} {:answer/id #uuid "9f341b2f-f73f-57f8-9565-0922cd0dee9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367342} {:answer/id #uuid "f91f2315-a41a-5693-9f45-09f93132b066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367343} {:answer/id #uuid "3e571ba3-1fc9-52bc-a38b-8d8a46aefa34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57d49a75-850a-580c-911f-1b7521839673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367341} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e28e57c-d582-5079-8b43-c2947583f2bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5c98c0e9-7419-5d7e-b919-df2475fb8298" :question/math-academy-id "q-131025" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-2x}$ is a solution to the equation $(1+2x){y}^{″}+4x{y}^{′}-4y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={e}^{4-2x}$\n- $y(x)=x{e}^{-2x}$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{2}{{e}^{2x}}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{4-2x}={e}^{4}\\cdot {e}^{-2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c={e}^{4}.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=x{e}^{-2x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{2}{{e}^{2x}}=2\\cdot {e}^{-2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367345 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb5bafa6-ea18-5d1f-94f3-c9702413a2ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367347} {:answer/id #uuid "aec31c83-427c-5433-be3c-d3aaa1a170f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367348} {:answer/id #uuid "b9fee1a3-6f09-5ce5-b634-79b4cf65cc0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367349} {:answer/id #uuid "7f0a9e3e-145e-5e00-a5b0-538c19faaaa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367350} {:answer/id #uuid "a30660cf-2fd5-5002-800b-0597189e69d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9fee1a3-6f09-5ce5-b634-79b4cf65cc0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367349} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9a9a9d7-cbc3-5982-bbd6-90ac6d838ddf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e6f1fe4c-12f4-51c1-8c80-ca5bb6fcfc47" :question/math-academy-id "q-131027" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x/3}$ is a solution to the equation $6{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={e}^{(x-3)/3}$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{x}{3}{e}^{x/3}$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{5{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x/3}}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{(x-3)/3}={e}^{-1}\\cdot {e}^{x/3}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c={e}^{-1}.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{x}{3}{e}^{x/3}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{5{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x/3}}=5\\cdot {e}^{x-2x/3}=5{e}^{x/3}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367352 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Linear ODEs Using the Superposition Principle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d45716c-2fad-5726-95ee-c6bbc118d67c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367354} {:answer/id #uuid "5d218f3c-017a-5173-a17f-6b465daff3ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367355} {:answer/id #uuid "bc41caf8-bda8-5d39-b161-7900af7b7bf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367356} {:answer/id #uuid "e73c7757-1072-5625-8352-302ce70aa8ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367357} {:answer/id #uuid "56a7aad0-4f03-5115-851a-6114b8fa377a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d45716c-2fad-5726-95ee-c6bbc118d67c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367354} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f9d91eb-ad21-5686-9de6-f6a5e523e1ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5af618e0-efed-5d52-a5fe-f92221b7400c" :question/math-academy-id "q-131038" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-3t}$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={e}^{-2t}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(t)=2{e}^{-3t}+3{e}^{-2t}$\n- $y(t)=(4+{e}^{t}){e}^{-3t}$\n- $y(t)=5{e}^{-3t}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(t)$ and ${y}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(t)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(t)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-3t}$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={e}^{-2t}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}t{e}^{-2t}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(t)=2{e}^{-3t}+3{e}^{-2t}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=2,{c}_{2}=3.$\n\n- The function $y(t)=(4+{e}^{t}){e}^{-3t}=4{e}^{-3t}+{e}^{-2t}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=4,{c}_{2}=1.$\n\n- The function $y(t)=5{e}^{-3t}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=5,{c}_{2}=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367387 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115334 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf19e2ee-7c42-58b0-9254-5a44f315288a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Block Diagonal and Block Triangular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ef9cea3-974f-528a-8a79-d769560f6f23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367389} {:answer/id #uuid "2d4039a2-724f-5084-a178-230ee24c7ab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367390} {:answer/id #uuid "79cfabe6-5e8a-5586-b435-8d612b308e2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367391} {:answer/id #uuid "99b24c2d-4353-53c8-a796-8d1caa274c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367392} {:answer/id #uuid "e9ecd1cb-4821-5b77-bb54-6d54d427e49e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ef9cea3-974f-528a-8a79-d769560f6f23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367389} :answer-field/id #uuid "de0f3f59-de03-58a3-8a89-5751a78710ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba25631a-3eab-56f2-a3d7-d0c6da834d90" :question/math-academy-id "q-131447" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are block diagonal?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A block diagonal matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and all the other blocks contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is a block diagonal matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $C$ is a block diagonal matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367881 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124570 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bdcb378-9b17-5091-8e17-1a127ff923c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367883} {:answer/id #uuid "19754d07-0117-5cd5-9bef-485b95bb1895" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367884} {:answer/id #uuid "3632ca5c-ec54-5176-ae55-ab8f0ccda827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367885} {:answer/id #uuid "8453f8ea-6574-5d78-acbc-6fa00e579336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367886} {:answer/id #uuid "65455843-bb52-543e-9930-2c27a520e751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19754d07-0117-5cd5-9bef-485b95bb1895" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367884} :answer-field/id #uuid "98cace57-36bb-5a76-b3a7-cc33bc716166" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "883c276b-11e1-503d-959e-3a4c3a5e61c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-163777" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈3+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=-2$ and $det(A)=-7$\n- $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n- $a$ is a saddle point of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-2$ and\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-7.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-2<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-7<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367888 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124570 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cd99cd5-ac0d-53d1-865b-83d8b73fd3a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367890} {:answer/id #uuid "34b19b11-5aa1-505b-9f36-6f2d94b4f32b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367891} {:answer/id #uuid "e4ebc83e-3b44-54d2-95a8-0de91882ddf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367892} {:answer/id #uuid "afa00808-4640-5931-99bc-fd53f585d867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367893} {:answer/id #uuid "9f895f5a-45c6-5e98-96ad-9e05f2f12b6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4ebc83e-3b44-54d2-95a8-0de91882ddf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367892} :answer-field/id #uuid "f63163a1-bf8c-56be-b743-efedda7d3b91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "61ffb698-9cc2-5e02-b6d9-afec7d1f3422" :question/math-academy-id "q-163790" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-7+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)>0$\n- $A$ is positive-definite\n- $a$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -4\\end{aligned}|+4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-5(11)-2(-4)+4(10) \\\\  & =-7<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-5<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}|=11>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-7<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a34d342c-e7fc-581b-b3d1-ed6a49f1a62f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Boundary Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc469ea1-79c9-52d1-81a6-2552c0d7b7a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368245} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f5c5e9-8572-5a90-8258-c0b86e590584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368246} {:answer/id #uuid "286b767a-7e4b-5004-96bc-dfa2fa2a10d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368247} {:answer/id #uuid "1deef986-7713-552b-bba4-ed0a01c44300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368248} {:answer/id #uuid "fff00a93-c9be-561a-844f-19a7dab63479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "286b767a-7e4b-5004-96bc-dfa2fa2a10d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368247} :answer-field/id #uuid "8caa9690-b35c-5a66-a23b-26780bada7bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "67ebcbc8-9a0b-5eef-bc58-dbb711498ad0" :question/math-academy-id "q-338536" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a boundary value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-y={x}^{2},\\,y(0)=1,\\,y(2)=3$\n- ${y}^{″}-4y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=2$\n- ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y={e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at *different* locations in the domain of $y,$ then this is a **boundary value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=2$).\n\n- The second system is not a boundary value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the *same* location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$). This is an **initial value problem.**\n\n- The third system is not a boundary value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified."}]}
